"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[41997],{141997:n=>{n.exports="# Snowflake Scripting — Control Flow, Cursors, and Error Handling\n\nControl flow, cursors, error handling, dynamic SQL, and SYSTEM$\\* functions → DBSQL equivalents. See `05a-scripting-procs-udfs.md` for stored procedures, UDFs, and variables.\n\n## SNOWFLAKE\n\n### RESULTSET / CURSOR — Snowflake's table-variable equivalent\n\nSnowflake does not have a dedicated table-variable type. Instead, Snowflake Scripting uses **`RESULTSET`** and **`CURSOR`** types to hold intermediate result sets.\n\n**Single-query scope** (result used inside one SELECT): replace with a CTE.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake Scripting\nDECLARE rs RESULTSET DEFAULT (SELECT id, val FROM source);\nBEGIN\n  ... -- rs is used in one downstream statement\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL\nWITH rs AS (SELECT id, val FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source)\nSELECT * FROM rs;\n```\n\n**Multi-statement scope** (result referenced across multiple statements): replace with a temporary view.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake: RESULTSET consumed by multiple statements via cursor\nDECLARE\n  rs RESULTSET DEFAULT (SELECT id, val FROM source WHERE active = TRUE);\n  cur CURSOR FOR rs;\nBEGIN\n  FOR rec IN cur DO\n    UPDATE other SET flag = 1 WHERE id = rec.id;\n  END FOR;\n  SELECT COUNT(*) FROM TABLE(RESULT_SCAN(LAST_QUERY_ID()));\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL: temp view (session-scoped, visible across statements)\nCREATE OR REPLACE TEMPORARY VIEW rs AS\nSELECT id, val FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source WHERE active = TRUE;\n\nUPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.other SET flag = 1 WHERE id IN (SELECT id FROM rs);\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM rs;\n```\n\n### Control flow\n\n| Snowflake Scripting                                            | DBSQL                                                               | Notes                                                                                                                                                                           |\n| -------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `IF (x = 1) THEN ... END IF;`                                  | `IF x = 1 THEN ... END IF;`                                         | Parentheses around the condition are optional in both                                                                                                                           |\n| `IF (x = 1) THEN ... ELSEIF (x = 2) THEN ... ELSE ... END IF;` | `IF x = 1 THEN ... ELSEIF x = 2 THEN ... ELSE ... END IF;`          | Same keyword form                                                                                                                                                               |\n| `WHILE (i < 10) DO ... END WHILE;`                             | `WHILE i < 10 DO ... END WHILE;`                                    | Same                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| `FOR i IN 1 TO 10 DO ... END FOR;` (integer range)             | `FOR row AS SELECT id FROM range(1, 11) DO ... row.id ... END FOR;` | DBSQL `FOR [row AS] <query>` is the idiomatic replacement. Refer to columns via the loop variable (`row.id`); `range(start, end + 1)` covers Snowflake's inclusive upper bound. |\n| `FOR rec IN cursor_name DO ... END FOR;` (cursor iteration)    | `FOR rec AS SELECT ... DO ... END FOR;`                             | DBSQL `FOR [var AS] query DO` is a direct analog. Use `OPEN`/`FETCH`/`CLOSE` only when row-handle control is required — see the cursor section below.                           |\n| `REPEAT ... UNTIL cond END REPEAT;`                            | Rewrite as `WHILE` (with care — REPEAT runs body before checking)   |                                                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `LOOP ... BREAK ... END LOOP;`                                 | `loop_label: LOOP ... LEAVE loop_label; ... END LOOP loop_label;`   | DBSQL requires loop labels; use `LEAVE` instead of `BREAK`                                                                                                                      |\n| `BREAK;`                                                       | `LEAVE <label>;`                                                    | Label required in DBSQL                                                                                                                                                         |\n| `CONTINUE;`                                                    | `ITERATE <label>;`                                                  | Label required                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| `RETURN expr;` from procedure                                  | Not supported for scalar return in DBSQL procedures                 | Use OUT parameter, or convert procedure to UDF                                                                                                                                  |\n| `LET v := expr;` / `v := expr;`                                | `SET v = expr;`                                                     | After `DECLARE v TYPE;` — inside compound statements use `SET` (no `VAR` keyword)                                                                                               |\n\n### Example: IF / ELSE / ELSEIF\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake Scripting\nBEGIN\n  LET count INT := (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t);\n  IF (count > 0) THEN\n    UPDATE t SET status = 'active';\n  ELSEIF (count = 0) THEN\n    INSERT INTO t VALUES (1, 'inactive');\n  ELSE\n    INSERT INTO t VALUES (-1, 'error');\n  END IF;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL\nBEGIN\n  DECLARE cnt INT DEFAULT 0;\n  SET cnt = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM my_catalog.my_schema.t);\n  IF cnt > 0 THEN\n    UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.t SET status = 'active';\n  ELSEIF cnt = 0 THEN\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (1, 'inactive');\n  ELSE\n    INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (-1, 'error');\n  END IF;\nEND;\n```\n\n### IF EXISTS patterns — two distinct cases\n\n**Case 1 — Row-presence check** (checks whether any row exists in a table):\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake Scripting\nBEGIN\n  LET has_rows BOOLEAN := (SELECT COUNT(*) > 0 FROM staging_periods);\n  IF (has_rows) THEN\n    CALL do_something();\n  END IF;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL (COUNT into a variable, then test)\nBEGIN\n  DECLARE v_count INT DEFAULT 0;\n  SET v_count = (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM my_catalog.my_schema.staging_periods);\n  IF v_count > 0 THEN\n    CALL my_catalog.my_schema.do_something();\n  END IF;\nEND;\n```\n\nUse the variable form as the primary translation — it keeps the control-flow predicate simple and mirrors how the source Snowflake was structured.\n\n**Case 2 — Object-existence check** (checks whether a table/procedure/etc. exists):\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake: typically via INFORMATION_SCHEMA\nBEGIN\n  IF (EXISTS (\n    SELECT 1 FROM my_db.INFORMATION_SCHEMA.TABLES\n    WHERE TABLE_NAME = 'STAGING' AND TABLE_SCHEMA = 'MY_SCHEMA'\n  )) THEN\n    DROP TABLE my_db.my_schema.staging;\n  END IF;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL: use built-in IF EXISTS on the DDL itself\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS my_catalog.my_schema.staging;\n```\n\n**Distinguishing the two patterns**: if the subquery selects from `INFORMATION_SCHEMA.*`, it is Case 2 (object-existence) — translate to `DROP ... IF EXISTS` / `CREATE ... IF NOT EXISTS`. If the subquery selects from a user table, it is Case 1 (row-presence) — translate to a COUNT-into-variable check.\n\n### WHILE loop\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake Scripting\nDECLARE i INT DEFAULT 0;\nWHILE (i < 10) DO\n  INSERT INTO t VALUES (i);\n  i := i + 1;\nEND WHILE;\n\n-- DBSQL\nDECLARE i INT DEFAULT 0;\nWHILE i < 10 DO\n  INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (i);\n  SET i = i + 1;\nEND WHILE;\n```\n\n### FOR integer-range rewrite\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nFOR i IN 1 TO 10 DO\n  INSERT INTO t VALUES (i);\nEND FOR;\n\n-- DBSQL (preferred — FOR over range())\n-- Columns from the query are accessed via the loop-variable qualifier (row.id here).\nFOR row AS SELECT id FROM range(1, 11) DO\n  INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (row.id);\nEND FOR;\n\n-- DBSQL (fallback — WHILE counter)\nDECLARE i INT DEFAULT 1;\nWHILE i <= 10 DO\n  INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.t VALUES (i);\n  SET i = i + 1;\nEND WHILE;\n```\n\nSnowflake `FOR i IN <low> TO <high> DO` is inclusive on both ends — `range(low, high + 1)` produces the same values.\n\nFor `FOR ... REVERSE`:\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nFOR i IN REVERSE 10 TO 1 DO\n  ...\nEND FOR;\n\n-- DBSQL\nDECLARE i INT DEFAULT 10;\nWHILE i >= 1 DO\n  ...\n  SET i = i - 1;\nEND WHILE;\n```\n\n### REPEAT / UNTIL rewrite\n\nSnowflake `REPEAT ... UNTIL cond` evaluates the condition at the END — the body runs at least once. Translate carefully to WHILE.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nREPEAT\n  DELETE FROM t WHERE id = i;\n  i := i + 1;\nUNTIL (i > 10)\nEND REPEAT;\n\n-- DBSQL (preserve \"run at least once\" semantics)\nDECLARE done BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE;\nWHILE NOT done DO\n  DELETE FROM my_catalog.my_schema.t WHERE id = i;\n  SET i = i + 1;\n  IF i > 10 THEN SET done = TRUE; END IF;\nEND WHILE;\n```\n\n### Cursors (literal translation)\n\nCursors are an anti-pattern — prefer set-based rewrites (see `references/snowflake/04-select-and-dml.md`). Use literal translation only when row-by-row semantics are strictly required. Cursor syntax requires **DBR 18.1+ / SQL Serverless**. Not supported in notebooks or jobs on classic/serverless compute clusters.\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake Scripting (FOR rec IN cursor DO)\nDECLARE\n  my_cursor CURSOR FOR SELECT id FROM source_table;\nBEGIN\n  FOR rec IN my_cursor DO\n    UPDATE other_table SET processed = 1 WHERE id = rec.id;\n  END FOR;\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL (explicit OPEN / FETCH / CLOSE loop on SQL warehouse)\nBEGIN\n  DECLARE v_id INT;\n  DECLARE done INT DEFAULT 0;\n  DECLARE cur CURSOR FOR SELECT id FROM my_catalog.my_schema.source_table;\n  DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND SET done = 1;\n\n  OPEN cur;\n  fetch_loop: LOOP\n    FETCH cur INTO v_id;\n    IF done = 1 THEN LEAVE fetch_loop; END IF;\n    UPDATE my_catalog.my_schema.other_table SET processed = 1 WHERE id = v_id;\n  END LOOP fetch_loop;\n  CLOSE cur;\nEND;\n```\n\n> The cursor syntax above is complete. Load `skills/writing-sql/sql-scripting.md` for general scripting constructs (variables, IF/ELSE, WHILE, error handlers) — it does not document cursor syntax.\n\n### Error handling (EXCEPTION → DECLARE HANDLER)\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake Scripting\nBEGIN\n  INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, amount) VALUES (1, 100.00);\nEXCEPTION\n  WHEN OTHER THEN\n    LET err_msg STRING := SQLERRM;\n    RAISE;    -- bare RAISE re-raises the current exception\nEND;\n\n-- DBSQL\nBEGIN\n  DECLARE err_msg STRING;\n  DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION\n  BEGIN\n    GET DIAGNOSTICS CONDITION 1 err_msg = MESSAGE_TEXT;\n    SIGNAL USER_RAISED_EXCEPTION SET MESSAGE_TEXT = err_msg;\n  END;\n  INSERT INTO my_catalog.my_schema.orders (customer_id, amount) VALUES (1, 100.00);\nEND;\n```\n\n**Mapping table:**\n\n| Snowflake Scripting                        | DBSQL                                                               | Notes                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `EXCEPTION WHEN OTHER THEN ...`            | `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION BEGIN ... END`               | Handler declared at top of block                                                                                                                                            |\n| `EXCEPTION WHEN STATEMENT_ERROR THEN ...`  | `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER FOR SQLEXCEPTION`                             | DBSQL has less granular conditions                                                                                                                                          |\n| `EXCEPTION WHEN EXPRESSION_ERROR THEN ...` | same                                                                | Map to generic SQLEXCEPTION                                                                                                                                                 |\n| `SQLERRM`                                  | `MESSAGE_TEXT` via `GET DIAGNOSTICS CONDITION 1 msg = MESSAGE_TEXT` |                                                                                                                                                                             |\n| `SQLCODE`                                  | No direct equivalent — use `RETURNED_SQLSTATE`                      | Snowflake `SQLCODE` is a **5-digit signed integer**; DBSQL exposes only the 5-char string SQLSTATE. Numeric comparisons must be rewritten as string comparisons or removed. |\n| `SQLSTATE`                                 | `RETURNED_SQLSTATE`                                                 | Same 5-char format in both dialects                                                                                                                                         |\n| `RAISE <user_declared_exception>`          | `SIGNAL USER_RAISED_EXCEPTION SET MESSAGE_TEXT = ...`               | DBSQL's documented built-in condition for user-defined errors. Custom `DECLARE ... EXCEPTION` in Snowflake has no DBSQL equivalent.                                         |\n| `RAISE;` (bare re-raise inside handler)    | `RESIGNAL;`                                                         | Re-raises the currently handled exception                                                                                                                                   |\n\n**CONTINUE vs EXIT handlers:**\n\n- `DECLARE EXIT HANDLER` — exits the block when triggered (equivalent to Snowflake EXCEPTION which exits the BEGIN block)\n- `DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER` — resumes after the failing statement (no Snowflake equivalent; Snowflake always exits to EXCEPTION)\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL: CONTINUE handler (used in cursor loops)\nBEGIN\n  DECLARE CONTINUE HANDLER FOR NOT FOUND SET done = 1;\n  -- ... cursor FETCH loop uses this to detect end-of-data\nEND;\n```\n\n### RAISE → SIGNAL\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake\nRAISE;                                   -- bare re-raise (only valid inside EXCEPTION handler)\nRAISE my_custom_error;                   -- must be DECLAREd as an EXCEPTION earlier\n\n-- DBSQL\nRESIGNAL;                                -- bare re-raise (inside a handler)\nSIGNAL USER_RAISED_EXCEPTION SET MESSAGE_TEXT = 'Custom error message';   -- fresh user-defined error\n```\n\nSnowflake custom exception declarations (`DECLARE my_err EXCEPTION (-20001, 'text')`) have no DBSQL equivalent. Translate `RAISE my_err;` to `SIGNAL USER_RAISED_EXCEPTION SET MESSAGE_TEXT = '...'` carrying the same text (DBSQL's documented built-in condition for user-defined errors).\n\nNote: Snowflake does **not** allow raising built-in exceptions like `STATEMENT_ERROR` — those appear only in `WHEN` clauses when catching. If the source shows `RAISE STATEMENT_ERROR`, treat it as a mistake and re-raise with bare `RAISE;` / `RESIGNAL;`.\n\n### Dynamic SQL\n\n```sql\n-- Snowflake Scripting\nLET sql_stmt STRING := 'SELECT * FROM ' || :table_name;\nEXECUTE IMMEDIATE :sql_stmt;\n-- With positional bind:\nEXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'SELECT * FROM t WHERE id = ?' USING (1);\n-- With named bind:\nEXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'SELECT * FROM t WHERE id = :id' USING (id => 1);\n\n-- DBSQL\nDECLARE sql_stmt STRING DEFAULT CONCAT('SELECT * FROM ', table_name);\nEXECUTE IMMEDIATE sql_stmt;\n-- Positional bind (? placeholders; arguments in USING are positional):\nEXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'SELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.t WHERE id = ?' USING 1;\n```\n\n**Bind differences:**\n\n- DBSQL `EXECUTE IMMEDIATE` supports **both** positional `?` and named `:name` markers — but all markers in a given statement must be the same style (no mixing).\n- Snowflake `USING (name => value)` named-bind syntax → DBSQL `USING value AS name` (alias form). Example:\n\n  ```sql\n  -- Snowflake\n  EXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'SELECT * FROM t WHERE id = :id' USING (id => 1);\n\n  -- DBSQL (named)\n  EXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'SELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.t WHERE id = :id' USING 1 AS id;\n\n  -- DBSQL (positional — also valid)\n  EXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'SELECT * FROM my_catalog.my_schema.t WHERE id = ?' USING 1;\n  ```\n\n- Translation is flexible — preserve the source's named-vs-positional choice, or pick the clearer form.\n\n**Dynamic identifier interpolation**: use DBSQL `IDENTIFIER()` to parameterize table / column names safely:\n\n```sql\n-- DBSQL\nDECLARE tbl STRING DEFAULT 'my_catalog.my_schema.orders';\nEXECUTE IMMEDIATE 'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM IDENTIFIER(:t)' USING tbl AS t;\n```\n\n> For full `EXECUTE IMMEDIATE` and `IDENTIFIER()` syntax, load `writing-sql/dynamic-sql.md`.\n\n### SYSTEM$\\* functions\n\nSnowflake has many `SYSTEM$*` functions for pipeline status, wait, change detection, etc. Most have no DBSQL equivalent.\n\n| Snowflake                                            | DBSQL                                                                  |\n| ---------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SYSTEM$WAIT(seconds)`                               | No equivalent — remove or TODO                                         |\n| `SYSTEM$LAST_CHANGE_COMMIT_TIME('catalog.schema.t')` | `SELECT MAX(timestamp) FROM (DESCRIBE HISTORY my_catalog.my_schema.t)` |\n| `SYSTEM$PIPE_STATUS('my_pipe')`                      | No equivalent — use Workflow UI / DLT pipeline API                     |\n| `SYSTEM$STREAM_HAS_DATA('s')`                        | Query `table_changes(...)` and check `count(*) > 0`                    |\n| `SYSTEM$QUERY_REFERENCE('name')`                     | No equivalent                                                          |\n| `SYSTEM$CANCEL_QUERY(query_id)`                      | No SQL equivalent — use the Databricks jobs / queries REST API         |\n\nMost `SYSTEM$*` calls are procedural scaffolding that becomes unnecessary after migration to Workflows / DLT. Remove and re-express the intent via the Databricks pipeline orchestration primitives.\n"}}]);
